      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of 18 and 42, we first need to determine both the GCD and the LCM.\n\n1. **Finding the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD):**\n    - We start by finding the prime factorization of the two numbers:\n        - The prime factors of 18 are \\(2^1 \\times 3^2\\).\n        - The prime factors of 42 are \\(2^1 \\times 3^1 \\times 7^1\\).\n    - The GCD is found by taking the lowest power of each common prime factor:\n        - The common factors are \\(2^1\\) and \\(3^1\\).\n        - Therefore, the GCD is \\(2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\\).\n\n2. **Finding the Least Common Multiple (LCM):**\n    - The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor that appears in the factorization of either number.\n    - From the prime factorizations above:\n        - The highest power of 2 is \\(2^1\\).\n        - The highest power of 3 is \\(3^2\\).\n        - The highest power of 7 is \\(7^1\\).\n    - Therefore, the LCM is \\(2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\\).\n\n3. **Calculating the Product:**\n    - Finally, we calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n    \\[\n    \\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n    \\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is $\\boxed{756}$.",
